"""`decryption-policy`'s four finding models, their four query-link models, and the four new
control types the `AlterField` below widens the choices list for.

FOUR MINTS IN ONE MIGRATION, which is the most this catalogue has taken at once. The
`AlterField` on `Control.control_type` is GENERATED and not hand-written: that literal has to
match the enum exactly, a transcription slip in it is invisible until a control cannot be
saved, and `makemigrations --check --dry-run` is the only thing that catches one. It answers
`No changes detected` against this file.

`max_length` STAYS 64 AND IS NOT WIDENED HERE, and the longest value this migration adds is
`ssl_decryption_vendor_exclusion_optout` at 38 characters. That is the fact worth leaving in
the tree: the field was 32 until 2026-11-01, so this domain could not have been registered the
day before - `makemigrations` REFUSES rather than truncating when a `choices` value overflows
(`fields.E009: 'max_length' is too small to fit the longest value in 'choices'`) and would have
generated nothing at all.

TWO GRAINS OVER ONE PAN-OS OBJECT, which is why there are four finding models and not three.
`DecryptionPolicyRuleFinding` names a rule that EXISTS and is wrong;
`DecryptionRulebaseCoverageFinding` names a virtual system whose rulebase does not exist at all,
which stage 4 measured as the commonest state of this domain across all four lab appliances and
all twenty virtual systems. A predicate cannot compare against a row that is not there.

AND ONE OF THE FOUR HAS NO CONTROL TO PRODUCE IT. `SslDecryptionVendorExclusionOptOutFinding`
is the `lldp_global` state: PAN-DEC-004's two assertion records name two models, a control has
exactly one `target_model`, and splitting that catalogue id is not a build run's decision. The
model and its generator land together so that whoever splits it adds one catalog entry rather
than writing a generator from nothing.
"""

